## 3. Decisions and rationale

| Decision | Why |
|---|---|
| **Semantic YAML format**, not ASCII grids | LLMs make alignment/column errors editing ASCII maps. Named IDs + structure are robust to edit and easy to reason about. |
| **Not SVG as the source file** | Semantics get buried in attributes; LLMs editing SVG path strings is error-prone. The web tool *renders* to SVG. SVG *import* from Orlando's existing drawings can be added later. |
| **Shape primitives**: polygon, circle, path-with-width (corridors); arcs later | Rooms are not boxes. Storing a circle as a circle keeps it editable as one and legible to the model. |
| **Openings anchor to walls** (edge index + position along edge + height), not absolute coordinates | Dragging a vertex keeps doors/windows attached. The agent can say "north wall" without doing geometry. |
| **Elevation per space, height per opening** | It's a 3D platformer. Heights make ability requirements checkable (a window above jump reach needs telekinesis). Plan view + elevation layers is enough for ideation. |
| **Topology separate from geometry** | LLMs reason well about graphs ("key before locked door") and poorly about precise geometry. |
| **Typed gates referencing palette IDs** (`requires: ability:telekinesis`, `blocked_by: npc:emi`, `hidden`, `one_way`) | Enforces the fixed palette mechanically; enables validation. |
| **Notes are first-class, distinct from game entities**, with anchor + status | Notes carry designer intent and act as work items the agent can pick up ("trigger goes here…"). |
| **Palette, design guide and rubric are shared assets** | The web tool's drag-and-drop menu and the agent's allowed vocabulary must come from the same data so they never drift. |
| **Command pattern for all edits** (human and agent) with one linear history | Clear joint undo/redo; the history shows who did what. |
| **Transactions**: an agent proposal = one undo step | Undoing 40 micro-commands is useless. Also gives "reject proposal" for free. |
| **Linear history first**, no selective undo | Selectively undoing an earlier agent change under later edits gets complicated fast. Proposals as variants cover most of that need. |
| **Local server is the source of truth**; YAML is persistence + readable snapshot | Removes file-watch races between human and agent. |
| **Agent connects via MCP** (not by editing files) | Commands become typed tools with schemas; invalid references are rejected before being written. Validators and render become tools; palette/guide become resources. |
| **Small MCP tool surface**, batched commands | Every tool definition costs agent context; many granular tools make tool choice worse. |
| **`get_level` returns the whole level (or a region)** | LLMs reason better from one compact document than from dozens of tiny queries. |
| **Command logic in a core library**, called by the MCP server *and* a thin CLI | Keeps tests and scripts working without the server running. |
| **Design judgment lives in skills/context**, not MCP | MCP carries tools, not taste. The guide, rubric and workflow stay editable as markdown. |
| **Fallback: detect raw YAML edits and record them as one "raw edit" command** | The agent (or Orlando) may edit the file by hand anyway; history stays intact, just coarser. |

### Honest assessment of the agent's role

- **Good at:** breadth of combinations from a fixed palette; reasoning about a beat's purpose and pacing; consistency with dungeon structure and player abilities; fast variations on a chosen direction; critique when validators hand it data.
- **Weak at:** feel and timing; precise 3D spatial reasoning (describes layouts that sound coherent but don't quite work); vague words like "medium intensity" unless defined; tends toward generic, overstuffed designs if unconstrained.
- **So:** it is an idea generator and design sparring partner. Nothing it proposes ships without being grayboxed and played in Unity.

