## 12. Roadmap

**Phase 0 — Format & palette by hand (with Orlando).**
Finalize the level format spec and the palette file. Hand-encode the three-room scenario and at least one more real dungeon section. Hand-encoding real designs will surface format problems before any code exists. Draft the intensity rubric and get reference rooms from Orlando.

**Phase 1 — Core library + CLI.**
Level model with schema validation, commands with inverses, history and transactions, `check_reachability`. Thin CLI for tests. Acceptance tests from section 9.

**Phase 2 — Headless MCP server.**
Expose the core via MCP (tools + resources from section 8). Write the agent skill from section 10. Run scenarios 2, 3, 5 and 7 **with no web UI**. If the agent is useful at this stage, the canvas is worth building.

**Phase 3 — `render_map` + `check_structure`.**
The agent's "contact sheet": PNG render with floors and proposal overlays.

**Phase 4 — Web authoring tool.**
Canvas on the same server: draw shapes, wall-anchored openings, elevation layers, palette drag-and-drop, notes, proposal overlays, undo/redo via the shared history. Live updates when the agent acts.

**Phase 5 — Unity handoff.**
Export reference images per floor + notes. Revisit a graybox importer only if manual reconstruction becomes the bottleneck.

**Later / maybe:** SVG import from existing drawings; arcs; side-section view; more validators (pacing metrics, backtracking distance).

